    "knowledge_point": "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist",
    "problem": "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)={e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}?$\n\nI. ${f}_{y}(1,-1)$ is defined\n\nII. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,0)$\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(1,1)$ is defined",
    "worked_solution": "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}}{2\\sqrt{x-y}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}\\cdot \\frac{-1}{2\\sqrt{x-y}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}}{2\\sqrt{x-y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{1-(-1)}}={e}^{\\sqrt{2}},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,-1)=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1-(-1)}}}{2\\sqrt{1-(-1)}}=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}}}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,0)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,0)={e}^{\\sqrt{1-0}}={e}^{1}=e,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,0) & =\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1-0}}}{2\\sqrt{1-0}}=\\frac{{e}^{1}}{2\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{e}{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,0) & =-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1-0}}}{2\\sqrt{1-0}}=-\\frac{{e}^{1}}{2\\sqrt{1}}=-\\frac{e}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(1,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(1,1)=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1-1}}}{2\\sqrt{1-1}}=\\frac{{e}^{0}}{2\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II  only'.",
